         # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECRETS — nothing in this block may ever be committed.
#
# `.env.local` files in this repo hold POG_SIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY, the sole trust
# root of the quota system: whoever holds it can sign an arbitrary allocation
# for an arbitrary address.  The root `.env` holds the deployer PRIVATE_KEY and
# BASESCAN_API_KEY.  The patterns are deliberately broad — a new `.env.staging`
# should be ignored by default rather than by remembering to add it.
#
# Committed templates carry NO values: `.env.example`, `.env.production.example`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example

*.key
*.pem
*.keystore

# ─── Dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/

# ─── Foundry build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cache/
out/
foundry.lock

# Broadcast logs: keep real deployment receipts (they are the record of what is
# live on which chain), drop local-anvil and dry-run noise.
/broadcast/*/31337/
/broadcast/**/dry-run/

# Standard-JSON-input bundles for explorer verification. ~1.4 MB of flattened
# sources regenerable by `forge verify-contract --show-standard-json-input`, so
# the repository is not the right place to keep them. The instructions beside
# them ARE worth keeping, because the constructor arguments in there were read
# back off chain rather than assumed.
/verify-out/*.std.json

# ─── Next.js / frontend build output ─────────────────────────────────────────
.next/
build/
.vercel/
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo

# ─── Static analysis output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `slither --json` is ~8 MB of AST-derived detail that regenerates in ~15 s and
# goes stale the moment a contract changes. The triage that matters lives in
# slither-baseline.json; reproduce the raw report with
# `slither src --json slither.json`.
slither.json
slither-run.txt
# Ad-hoc dumps under other names. Two of these — 12.5 MB and 9 MB — were
# sitting untracked while the triage claimed the raw JSON was ignored, i.e. one
# `git add .` from a 21 MB commit.
.slither-*.json
slither-*.log
# NOT ignored: slither-baseline.json, the distilled (detector, file, scope)
# set that scripts/checkSlitherFindings.mjs gates §5.7 against. It is ~20 KB,
# it is the thing a re-run is compared to, and it has to be in the tree.

# ─── Captured tool output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Redirected stdout from forge/tsc/eslint runs. Four of these were tracked —
# including a `check.txt` whose entire content was the word "hi" — so a public
# clone shipped one machine's console scrollback. Every one regenerates from the
# command that made it, and a committed copy is stale from the next commit on.
gasreport.txt
tsc-out.txt
lint-out.txt
check.txt
*-out.txt

# ─── Local scratch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anvil fork snapshots and one-off scratch files from debugging sessions.
.fork-*.json
.tmp_*
*.log

# A one-off query script, dot-prefixed so it sorts with config and therefore
# read as config — it sat untracked AND unignored, one `git add -A` from being
# committed. It holds no credential (it reads soat-frontend/.env.local at run
# time, which is the right shape), so this entry is tidiness rather than an
# incident. Named because the near-miss is the point: a dotfile that is a
# SCRIPT is the one shape this section did not cover.
#
# Deliberately the exact name, not `.*.mjs`, which is the opposite call from
# the SECRETS block at the top. There, broad is right because a missed
# `.env.staging` is a leaked key. Here the stakes invert: nothing dot-prefixed
# and executable is tracked today, but `.prettierrc.mjs` and friends are
# ordinary names for files that MUST be, and the note at the foot of this file
# is a long account of how quietly git drops a new file that matches an ignore
# rule. Buying scratch-file tidiness with that trap is a bad trade.
.wallets.mjs

# The watcher's resume point, harvested hook set and STATE-06 window. Per
# deployment, not per repository: committing one machine's cursor would make
# every other machine rescan or, worse, skip the blocks in between.
monitoring/.watch-state*.json

# ─── OS / editor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

scripts/.drill-state.json

# Q1 drill working files: the Safe address and timings are published in
# the drill log once the run is scored, but the collected
# signatures are other people's and have no business in git.
scripts/.q1-state.json
scripts/.q1-request.json
scripts/.q1-signatures.json

# PoG rate rotation working files (scripts/rotateGasRate.mjs). Same reasoning as
# the drill files above: the request is reproducible and the signatures are other
# people's. The request also carries a live `expiresAt`, so a committed copy
# would be a stale instruction that looks current.
scripts/.rate-request.json
scripts/.rate-signatures.json

# Real Safe signer addresses + their proof-of-control signatures (PM-D4).
# Template is safe-owners.example.json.
safe-owners.json

# Generated by scripts/genVerifyInput.mjs for the Blockscout form. Reproducible
# from the tree, ~660 KB of solc input, and stale the moment src/ changes.
verify-input/

# docs/RESEARCH_BRIEF_zh.md used to be ignored here, on the reasoning that the
# published spec is the README and a second document covering the same ground
# could disagree with it in public. Tracking it instead, because ignoring it did
# not prevent the disagreement — it only hid it. The brief spent five days
# describing a retired chain, a Uniswap V4 pool and ETH pricing while the README
# was retargeted, and nothing reported the drift because nothing could see it.
#
# In the tree the two can at least be diffed. Note that no guard enforces their
# agreement: check/doc-symbols reads identifiers, not claims.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `vercel link` appends `.vercel` and `.env*` here, and BOTH must be deleted
# again rather than left as harmless duplicates. `.vercel/` is already covered
# at the top of this file, and `.env` / `.env.*` are too — but those come with
# `!.env.example` and `!.env.production.example`, and a bare `.env*` this far
# down silently overrides them, because the last matching pattern wins.
#
# The damage is exactly the two negated names, which is to say the two files
# the header above calls committed templates. With the bare rule in place
# `git check-ignore --no-index -v .env.example` answered `:128:.env*`; with it
# gone it answers `:14:!.env.example`. Nothing broke only because both were
# already tracked and git skips ignore rules for tracked paths — delete and
# re-add either one, or add it in a fresh worktree, and it goes back in
# silently unstaged.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
